This product includes software adapted from Hypercolor
(https://github.com/hyperb1iss/hypercolor), licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
Adapted files (layouts and packet encoders rewritten in Rust, not copied line by line):

- hypercolor-openrgb-sdk/src/parser.rs  -> crates/canvas-adapters/src/protocol.rs
  (OpenRGB controller-data field order; mode, zone and segment layouts; v4/v5 conditionals)
- hypercolor-openrgb-sdk/src/packet.rs  -> crates/canvas-adapters/src/protocol.rs
  (packet ids; UPDATELEDS, RESIZEZONE, SET_CLIENT_NAME, REQUEST_PROTOCOL_VERSION payloads;
  protocol version constants)
- hypercolor-openrgb-sdk/src/types.rs   -> crates/canvas-adapters/src/openrgb.rs
  (OpenRGB device-type and zone-type raw mapping)
- hypercolor-windows-pawnio/src/windows.rs -> crates/canvas-svc/src/pawnio.rs
  (PawnIO C entry-point names and argument shapes; THM_TCON_CUR_TMP decode constants)
- The DeviceAdapter trait shape (crates/canvas-proto/src/traits.rs) follows Hypercolor's
  DeviceBackend.

Run, not linked or modified:

- OpenRGB (https://codeberg.org/OpenRGB/OpenRGB), GPL-2.0. The installer downloads the official
  OpenRGB 1.1 Windows release, checks its SHA-256, and the service runs it unmodified as a
  separate process; canvas-rgb talks to it over OpenRGB's network SDK protocol. OpenRGB drives the
  motherboard ARGB headers, the RAM and the microphone. Its source is at the link above.
- PawnIO (https://github.com/namazso/PawnIO), the signed driver the fan service uses to reach the
  Super I/O chip. Installed separately by the owner; not shipped here.

Protocol knowledge from other people's open work (no code copied; cited in comments):

- Attack Shark X6: ATKsoft (https://github.com/grivia0/ATKsoft, MIT), the X6 read-back handshake,
  report layouts and checksums. Cross-checked against prodmutant/Attack-Shark-X3
  (https://github.com/prodmutant/Attack-Shark-X3), AlternativeLua/attack-shark-x3-driver
  (https://github.com/AlternativeLua/attack-shark-x3-driver), DavBfr/shark-x3
  (https://github.com/DavBfr/shark-x3) and libratbag (https://github.com/libratbag/libratbag).
- 8BitDo Ultimate 2: ascendedent/8bitdo-Linux-Software
  (https://github.com/ascendedent/8bitdo-Linux-Software), the configuration channel, the chunked
  profile read and the profile image layout. SDL's HIDAPI 8BitDo driver
  (https://github.com/libsdl-org/SDL, zlib) for the input report layout.
- The HTML effect format follows SignalRGB's (https://signalrgb.com) so effects written for it run
  here; no SignalRGB code is included.

Facts only, no code copied (cited in comments): register addresses for the Nuvoton NCT6687D-R
from the nct6687d Linux driver (GPL) and LibreHardwareMonitor's Nct677X.cs (MPL-2.0); PawnIO
LpcIO module ioctl names from PawnIO.Modules (GPL-2.0), used through PawnIO's IOCTL interface.

